<interrogationes unitas="anulus">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write an algebra routine once so it works over Z, Q, Laurent polynomials and Z/n?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus ANULUS_INTEGRORUM ANULUS_RATIONALIUM ANULUS_POLYNOMIORUM anulus_residuorum">Take
      a constans Anulus* and treat elements as opaque blocks of
      anulus-&gt;mensura bytes. Every operation is a function pointer
      that takes the descriptor first: anulus-&gt;adde(anulus, &amp;a,
      &amp;b, piscina, &amp;c). The ready rings are ANULUS_INTEGRORUM
      (elements Magnus), ANULUS_RATIONALIUM (Fractio),
      ANULUS_POLYNOMIORUM (Polynomium, text letter 't') and
      anulus_residuorum(n, piscina) for Z/n (elements i32). matrix is
      written this way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I call divisor_communis, divide_cum_residuo, compara_normam or signum on any ring?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus ANULUS_INTEGRORUM ANULUS_RATIONALIUM">No:
      they are optional and NIHIL where the ring lacks them, so test
      the pointer first. The three Euclidean hooks exist only for Z
      (NIHIL for Q, Z[t,t^-1] and Z/n). signum exists for Z and Q
      (and ordered extensio fields), NIHIL for Z[t,t^-1], Z/n and
      cyclotomia. Algorithms that need a missing hook return
      FALSUM.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make the ring Z/n, and how are its rings compared?
    <responsum symbolum="anulus_residuorum">anulus_residuorum(n,
      piscina) copies a descriptor into the piscina with the modulus
      in contextus and a title like "Z/7"; it returns NIHIL if n &lt; 2.
      corpus and integrum are VERUM only when n is prime. Matrices
      compare rings by POINTER, so two calls with n = 7 give two
      different rings: create one descriptor per modulus and share
      it.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I put an unreduced value like 9 into a Z/7 element directly?
    <responsum symbolum="anulus_residuorum Anulus">Do not: Z/n elements
      are i32 residues 0 &lt;= x &lt; n and aequalis compares the raw
      numbers, so a stored 9 is not equal to 2. Build elements with
      the ring's own operations, or with ex_chorda, which accepts any
      decimal integer (negative too) and reduces it modulo
      n.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does Anulus have an integrum flag separate from corpus?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus anulus_residuorum" via="lib/anulus.worklog.md">Bareiss
      elimination (determinant, rank, kernel) is only sound over an
      integral domain. Over composite Z/n a zero-divisor pivot gave
      WRONG answers instead of refusing: [0,3,3; 4,4,5; 5,5,5] over
      Z/6 came out det 0, truly 3. So matrix_determinans,
      matrix_gradus and matrix_nucleus refuse when integrum is FALSUM.
      Z is integrum but not corpus; Z/n is both exactly when n is
      prime.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I keep a ring element after resetting the scratch piscina it was computed in?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus piscina_notare piscina_reficere">Copy
      it with anulus-&gt;transcribe(anulus, &amp;a, piscina_durabilis,
      &amp;exitus) before piscina_reficere. transcribe makes a DEEP copy
      (Magnus limbs, Fractio parts and polynomial arrays are copied),
      so the value survives losing the source piscina. A plain struct
      copy would still point into the scratch memory.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the output of an anulus operation be the same element as an input?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus" via="lib/anulus.worklog.md">Yes:
      the wrappers read the inputs by value before writing the output,
      so adde(anulus, &amp;x, &amp;y, piscina, &amp;x) is safe and matrix
      relies on it. When an operation returns FALSUM (division not
      exact or by zero, polynomial exponent out of range, bad text)
      the output is left untouched.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does the parvum hook of an Anulus mean?
    <responsum symbolum="Anulus">parvum(a) is VERUM when the element
      lives entirely inside its struct with no external memory: Z when
      it fits in s64, Q when numerator and denominator both fit,
      Z[t,t^-1] only for the zero polynomial, Z/n always. Algorithms
      use it to take a scratch-free fast path.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
